WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.963
The decision, and why it is sticky.

2
00:00:02.243 --> 00:00:06.189
Choosing an embedding model feels like a small configuration choice.

3
00:00:06.469 --> 00:00:11.867
It isn’t, for one reason from lesson 1: vectors from different models are not comparable.

4
00:00:12.147 --> 00:00:18.440
Every document in your index was embedded by one model, and every query must be embedded by the same model.

5
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:25.056
Switching later means re-embedding the whole corpus, rebuilding the index, and re-running your evaluations.

6
00:00:25.336 --> 00:00:38.221
(One exception is worth knowing and verifying: Voyage AI says the models in its voyage-4 family produce compatible embeddings, so documents embedded with one can be searched with queries from another.

7
00:00:38.501 --> 00:00:42.064
That is a vendor claim about one family, not a general property.)

8
00:00:42.344 --> 00:00:45.523
Anthropic, notably, does not make an embedding model.

9
00:00:45.803 --> 00:00:56.256
Its embeddings guide says so directly, points to Voyage AI as one provider with a wide range of models, and also tells you to assess several vendors for your use case.

10
00:00:56.536 --> 00:01:06.605
The same advice holds whichever LLM you use for generation: the embedding model and the generator are independent choices, and mixing providers is normal.

11
00:01:06.885 --> 00:01:09.509
This lesson gives you the landscape, then the method.

12
00:01:09.789 --> 00:01:14.419
The landscape will age (model names change every few months); the method won’t.
